# Wire Desk

A scrolling news feed for GitHub Pages. Each story has its publisher, headline, short synopsis, photo and a link to the full report. No accounts or application server required.

Tuned for three things at once: **breaking local (Rhode Island)**, **breaking world**, **politics and elections** — plus a slow lane of longer reads that never competes with them.

## The main idea: pickup count

Most feed readers dedupe identical stories and throw the duplicates away. That's backwards — the duplicates *are* the signal. One outlet running something is a report. Five outlets running it inside an hour is an event.

So headlines get grouped into stories by word overlap, and each story shows how many outlets have it. Sort order weights pickup count alongside recency, and anything picked up by 3+ outlets within an hour gets a **picking up** badge and triggers an alert.

The timestamp shown is when the story *first* appeared, and the link goes to whoever broke it.

Two rules keep one outlet from owning the screen:

- **No source appears more than twice in any four consecutive rows.** A fast, prolific feed would otherwise take ten slots in a row purely by posting often. When a source hits its cap, the next-best *different* source is pulled up instead.
- **Feeds with no usable publication date are scored down.** Some feeds omit dates or format them oddly. Those items used to get stamped with the current time, which made everything from that outlet look like breaking news and pinned it to the top. Now they're marked and ranked below anything with a real timestamp.

### Home first

Every story lands in one of four tiers, and the tier multiplies its score:

| Tier | Weight | What's in it |
|---|---|---|
| Local | 2.4× | your town, alerts for your coordinates |
| State | 1.7× | your state's outlets and a statewide query |
| National | 1.15× | wires, politics, hazards, your own feeds |
| International | 0.45× | World and Español |

On top of the weighting there's a hard cap: **two international stories per 28 rows**, dropped in at positions 14 and 28. The rest are held back until the domestic stream runs dry. Tested with international making up 40% of the pool — still exactly two in the first 28.

To change the ratio, edit `BLOCK` in `applyQuota()`. It's one international slot per block, so 14 gives you 2 per 28; raise it for fewer, lower it for more.

Earthquakes are filtered to **magnitude 4.0 and up** (`QUAKE_MIN`). Below that is constant background noise.

## Alerts

Click "Turn on desktop alerts" and the browser asks permission. After that you get a notification when:

- a story hits 3+ outlets inside an hour, or
- a watchword shows up in a headline under 2 hours old

The tab title shows an unread count, and there's an optional chime. Nothing fires on first load, so you don't get blasted on open.

## Categories

| Category | What's in it |
|---|---|
| **Local** | WPRI 12, Rhode Island Current, Providence Journal, The Public's Radio, ecoRI, WBUR, a Google News query for RI/Providence/Woonsocket/North Smithfield, RI-only NWS weather alerts |
| **Wires** | Google News breaking (6h), Reuters, AP, BBC World |
| **World** | NPR, Guardian, Al Jazeera, DW, France 24, CBC, GDELT's global index |
| **Politics** | NPR Politics, The Hill, Politico, Guardian US politics, National Review, Reason, RealClearPolitics, plus a live elections/ballots/recount query |
| **Hazards** | USGS earthquakes, nationwide NWS alerts |
| **Social** | Whatever you add — Bluesky, Mastodon, Reddit |
| **Reads** | Hacker News front page, The Atlantic, Aeon, Quanta, Nautilus, Longreads, The Marginalian, MIT Tech Review, The Verge |

**On the politics lineup:** it deliberately spans left, center, and right so the pickup count means something. A story that five outlets across the spectrum are running is different from one that five outlets on the same side are running — and you can only see that if the lineup is mixed. Swap or add whatever you want; it's one line each in the `FEEDS` array.

**Reads are damped on purpose.** A longform essay is scored at about a third of its natural weight unless multiple sources pick it up, so an Atlantic piece never buries a fire in Woonsocket. Toggle the category off if you want a pure breaking-news board.

## Social watch

One box, three input shapes:

- `#wildfire` → Bluesky + Mastodon + Reddit for that tag
- `@handle` → follows one Bluesky account (reporters, agencies, your fire department)
- `plain words` → Bluesky keyword search + Reddit, catches posts with no hashtag at all

Bluesky is the reliable one — fully open read API, no key, no relay, never fails.

**X and Instagram aren't possible.** X went pay-per-use in February 2026 (roughly $0.005 per post read, no free tier, full-archive search is enterprise-only at $42k+/month). Instagram's Graph API only reads accounts you own and has no public hashtag search at all. Neither works from a static page even with your own login, because your browser won't send their cookies to your domain. For X coverage, add a Google News feed for `site:x.com` plus your terms.

## Your settings stick

Categories, watchwords, social watches, custom feeds, refresh interval and the chime save to `localStorage` on your own site. "Reset saved settings" clears them. If you preview the file inside a sandboxed frame, saving silently no-ops — that's expected, and it works normally once it's on Pages.

## On iPhone (Safari)

It works, and the layout is built for it — on a phone the controls collapse behind a **Controls** button at the top so you land on the news, not the settings panel.

Three iOS-specific things:

**Notifications need Home Screen.** Safari tabs can't do web notifications on iOS at all. Open the site, tap Share → **Add to Home Screen**, then open it from that icon. Now the alerts button works and you get real notifications. (`manifest.json` ships alongside `index.html` to make this work — keep both in the repo.)

**Nothing runs in the background.** iOS freezes timers the moment you switch apps or lock the screen, so auto-refresh stops and no alerts arrive while it's not on screen. When you come back it detects the missed refresh and catches up immediately. This is an iOS limit no web page can get around — background alerts would need a real server pushing to you, not a static page.

**Storage can expire.** Safari clears `localStorage` after about 7 days without visiting a site. Home Screen apps hold on longer, but if your setup ever resets after a long gap, that's why.

Practical read: on the phone it's an excellent *check it* tool. For alerts that reach you while you're cutting hair, leave it open on a laptop or an old tablet on the counter.

## It picks its own mode

There's no setup per device. On load it looks at the user agent and the pointer type and decides what it's running on:

- **Television** (Tizen, WebOS, Fire TV, Chromecast, Roku, consoles — or any screen 1280px+ with a pointer that can't hover, which means a remote) → display mode automatically
- **Phone** → controls collapsed behind a Controls button, big tap targets, no zoom-on-focus
- **Tablet / computer** → full rail

The rail shows what it detected, and there's a **Switch to display mode** button to force the other one. `?tv=1` and `?tv=0` in the URL override the detection permanently, which is what the TV link button uses.

There's also a watchdog: a tiny script written in old-style JavaScript runs first and checks four seconds later whether the main script ever started. If the browser was too old to even read the main script, you get a plain message saying so instead of a blank screen.

## Your area

Local means where you actually are, not a hardcoded state. Three ways in, in order of accuracy:

1. **Auto** — on first load it makes one quiet call to an IP geolocation service and uses whatever comes back. No permission prompt, no setup. It's approximate: IP location often lands on whatever town your ISP routes through, which can be a few towns off.
2. **ZIP code** — type five digits. Looked up through Zippopotam, no key needed. Reliable and precise enough for news and weather.
3. **Use my exact location** — asks the browser for GPS. Most accurate, and the only one that needs permission. Requires HTTPS, which GitHub Pages gives you.

Whatever it lands on drives three things:

- **Local headlines** — a Google News query built from your city and state, added automatically to the Local category
- **Weather** — current temperature and conditions in the header, refreshed every 15 minutes, from Open-Meteo (no key, works worldwide)
- **Two forecast rows in the stream**, sitting at positions 3 and 4 — high enough to see without scrolling, low enough that breaking news still leads. In the US these are NWS's own plain-English periods (Today / Tonight); elsewhere they're built from Open-Meteo with high, low and precipitation odds. They refresh every 30 minutes, sit outside the clustering pass so they never merge or get crowded out, and are never treated as alerts. They're hidden while a filter is active, so filtering still means what it says.
- **Weather alerts for your point** — NWS alerts filtered to your exact coordinates rather than the whole state, so you're not getting warnings for somewhere two hours away

Your choice is saved, so it only asks once. In TV display mode the weather shows in amber next to the clock.

Outside the US, the local news query and weather still work; NWS alerts don't, since that's a US service.

## Browse sources

A searchable directory of free news sources, no accounts anywhere. Type a country, a language, or an outlet name and tap to add.

**English international:** BBC, Guardian, Euronews, DW, France 24, Al Jazeera, CBC, ABC Australia, Channel News Asia, Times of India, Japan Times, Africanews, Kyiv Independent, plus country front pages for Nigeria, South Africa, Ireland and the Philippines.

**Español:** Colombia gets the most coverage — country front page plus El Tiempo, El Espectador, Semana, Blu Radio, Noticias Caracol and El Colombiano. Also México, España, Argentina, Chile, Perú, Venezuela, Ecuador, República Dominicana, Puerto Rico and Cuba, plus BBC Mundo, DW Español, France 24 Español, CNN en Español, Infobae, El País, Univision and Telemundo. They land in their own **Español** category so you can toggle the whole language on and off.

Most foreign sources route through Google News' per-country feed (`hl=es-419&gl=CO&ceid=CO:es` and equivalents) rather than each outlet's own RSS path. One endpoint that's always valid beats guessing dozens of paths that move around — which is exactly the failure mode that broke feeds earlier.

## Story feed and scrolling

The **For you** tab is one continuous feed using the existing local-first ranking and source diversity. Section tabs, watchwords, alerts, custom feeds, location and saved settings still work.

**Pause scroll / Start scroll** controls hands-free vertical scrolling. Choose Slow, Normal or Fast. Touching, typing or manually scrolling pauses movement for 12 seconds; hovering over a story pauses it while reading. At the bottom, the feed waits five seconds and returns to the top. Reduced-motion settings disable automatic scrolling by default. Background refreshes preserve the visible story position.

Publisher RSS descriptions provide the short synopsis. RSS media, image enclosures and embedded article images provide the photo. The scheduled feed builder also checks publisher preview metadata for a bounded number of stories missing either field. Google News headline lists and branding are not treated as article summaries or photos. A clearly labeled placeholder appears when the source has no usable photo; missing summaries are labeled rather than invented. Broken image URLs fall back to the same placeholder.

The feed builder saves `t` (title), `l` (link), `d` (date), `s` (synopsis), `i` (image URL), and `p` (Google News publisher) for each story in `feeds.json`. Older headline-only cached files still load. Summaries and photos persist in the device's stream cache and are enriched on refresh.

## Display mode

Add `?tv=1` or use **Switch to display mode** for a larger hands-free feed with images, synopses, a clock and pause/exit controls. The same continuous scrolling feed replaces the former video board and paginated headlines. The **Get TV display link** button includes categories and watchwords:

```
https://egiraldo1108.github.io/wire-desk/?tv=1&cats=local,wires,politics&words=fire,crash,outage
```

`?tv=0` forces the regular layout. `&every=300` sets the refresh interval. Existing saved video settings and old `video=` URLs are ignored; no video embeds or channel lookups run.

## Relays, in plain terms

Your browser only reads another site's data if that site says "anyone can read this." Most news sites never turned that on — not to block you, they just never bothered. So the page asks a free middleman service to fetch the feed and hand it back. That middleman is the relay.

There are nine relays, and **each keeps its own queue of three** rather than sharing one global limit. A single shared cap of three meant forty-odd feeds crawling through a three-wide door; nine relays at three apiece is up to 27 requests in flight while still never hammering any one proxy. Simulated with 45 feeds, that's a 9× speedup with load spread perfectly evenly.

Relays are also scored as they run. Wins count for them, failures count double against them, and any relay that fails four times without a single success is dropped for the rest of the session. The status line shows how many are up.

Two more things cut the load: feeds are tried **direct first** (a fair number send proper CORS headers and need no middleman, and those don't take a queue slot at all), and responses are cached for 90 seconds so a redraw never costs another round trip.

Three consequences:

1. **A `fail` is usually the relay, not the news site.** Tap any failed source in the health list to see the actual error. If it says HTTP 429 or a timeout, that's throttling — refresh. If it says "not a valid feed" or "no items" every single time, the URL is wrong and needs replacing.
2. **USGS, NWS, Hacker News, GDELT and Bluesky never fail** — they turned the setting on, so they're fetched direct with no middleman. That's your reliable core.
3. **Your own relay fixes it for good.** Ten minutes, free.

### Your own relay

Cloudflare Workers free tier. Sign up, new Worker, paste, deploy:

```js
export default {
  async fetch(req) {
    const target = new URL(req.url).searchParams.get('url');
    if (!target) return new Response('missing url', {status: 400});
    const r = await fetch(target, {headers: {'User-Agent': 'wire-desk/1.0'}});
    return new Response(r.body, {
      status: r.status,
      headers: {
        'Content-Type': r.headers.get('Content-Type') || 'text/plain',
        'Access-Control-Allow-Origin': '*'
      }
    });
  }
}
```

Put it first in `RELAYS` near the top of the script; leave the public ones as fallback.

## Adding feeds

In the UI, or permanently in the `FEEDS` array:

```js
{id:'wjar', name:'NBC 10', cat:'local', kind:'rss', url:'https://turnto10.com/feed'}
```

Finding links: `thesite.com/feed` or `/rss`; any subreddit takes `.rss` on the end; YouTube channels use `youtube.com/feeds/videos.xml?channel_id=…`; anything at all via `news.google.com/rss/search?q=your+terms`.

## Known rough edges

- **Some feed URLs will need swapping.** RSS paths move around, especially at local TV stations and Gannett papers. Tap a failed source to see why. Anything failing identically on every refresh is a bad URL, not a busy relay — replace it with a Google News query for that outlet: `news.google.com/rss/search?q=site:thepaper.com+when:2d`. That one endpoint is far more reliable than guessing a station's feed path, which is why several local sources already use it.
- **Copy failure report** puts every failing source, its URL and its exact error on your clipboard, ready to paste into a chat for help.
- **RSS runs 2–15 minutes behind.** Fine for awareness, not for anything time-critical.
- **Clustering is word-overlap, not meaning.** It occasionally merges two stories that share a lot of proper nouns, or misses a match when outlets word a headline very differently. Tuning knobs are `j >= 0.42` and `hit >= 2` in `add()` — raise for stricter grouping, lower for looser.
- Nothing scrapes or logs in anywhere. Public feeds and public APIs only.
- Repo needs both `index.html` and `manifest.json` for the iOS Home Screen behaviour.
